          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "**Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.**\n\nYour reference note's root (K21) is a category. To reach the specific code, you need to carry it to full length and check it against the official tables.\n\n**The two-step lookup.** The sources say you find a code in the Alphabetic Index, then verify it in the Tabular List. The Tabular List governs, because the index does not carry the instructional notes. Skipping the second step is a common way to end up with a wrong code. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n\n**Full length matters.** Most categories are not valid at three characters. Some require a seventh character, and the placeholder X holds an empty position so the seventh character lands in the right place. (Same source)\n\n**Where K21 sits.** K21 belongs to the digestive chapter, in the block for diseases of the esophagus, stomach and duodenum (K20 to K31). Esophagitis comes first, then reflux disease. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system*)\n\n**What the K21 guidance adds.** The coding summary on gastroesophageal reflux disease stresses the distinction between reflux disease and esophagitis. It says dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) is not routinely part of uncomplicated reflux disease, but is reported in addition where documented. Long-term drug therapy status and body mass index are coded from the factors-influencing-health chapter. (Source: *Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21 and the esophagitis distinction*)\n\n**What I could not find.** The passages I retrieved do not list the individual codes that sit under K21, and they do not include the Alphabetic Index entries that would lead to them. So I cannot tell you which full code matches your note. For that, use the official ICD-10-CM Tabular List and Index, or ask the person who wrote the note or a medical coder to confirm the code.",
